so we did this interview with a  psychiatrist a couple of months ago  called Trump's mental health is urgently  deteriorating and the topic was of  significant interest to our audience  about 350,000 views on YouTube I got at  least a hundred emails about that  interview and one really important  element of mental health professionals  making comments about what they see is  concerning in terms of Donald Trump's  behavior is that there is this sort of  conventional wisdom that you often hear  that you need to meet someone in person  in order to diagnose them that's true  for issuing a formal diagnosis but as  many mental health professionals have  explained to us over the last few months  there is enough audio-visual evidence  that mental health professionals can use  now to observe someone like president so  that they can make a pretty good  assessment of what might be going on  with one's cognitive state and potential  month mental illness or other  instabilities that one may be  experiencing and again it wouldn't be  something that would be used for a  formal diagnosis that would get an  insurance company to for example cover  SSRIs to diagnose someone via the  television but for the purposes of  evaluating what's going on with our  president it's actually a relatively  fair to do something like that and the  bad news is that it seems to be getting  worse both trumps mental state and the  opinions of mental health professionals  about that mental state seem to be  getting worse and when you look at the  books that have been written so far  about the Trump White House's inner  workings they all paint a pretty similar  picture which is one of a very erratic  and unstable Donald Trump a Bob Woodward  wrote in his book fear about a quote  nervous breakdown Michael Wolfe wrote in  fire and fury about a similar situation  we had that anonymous New York Times  op-ed from a White House official which  said that there were deep troubles in  the administration facing Donald Trump  and  mental health professionals are finding  themselves between our sort of two  conflicting policies which is you've got  that Goldwater rule which says diagnosed  people with their authorization and with  a personal in person examination up  against the duty to warn that mental  health professionals have overwhelmingly  increasingly mental health professionals  are saying that the duty to warn has to  take priority and there are now dozens  of co-authors of the book the dangerous  case of Donald Trump and thousands of  mental health professionals who are  agreeing with the books conclusions as  part of an organization called the  National Coalition of concerned mental  health experts and the conclusions are  that Trump is truly dangerous and I'm  not going to get into all the details  the book is very comprehensive it  includes assessments of Trump's tweets  his responses to questions in real time  during interviews and during press  conferences his narcissism his  recklessness his impulsivity and their  argument is that this isn't just  something that makes for sort of weird  press conferences that the behavior of  Trump represents a real threat when you  have him interacting with world leaders  and having access to the nuclear codes  check out the list of other risk factors  that they cite access to weapons poor  relationship with a spouse or  significant other seeing oneself as a  victim lack of compassion lack of  concern over the consequences of harmful  acts this is Trump this is Trump plain  and simple and this is aside from as the  mental health professionals point out  the cognitive decline concerns that we  and others have talked about many times  it's really important to understand that  if you don't buy the cognitive decline  argument and there are people in our  audience who don't and and they have  their reasons for not buying it this is  separate the duty to warn that mental  health professionals are expressing is  not limited to the cognitive decline but  rather to what seemed to be the  fundamental sort of behavioural  personality issues that this man is  dealing with but on the side on the  other side you've got that  pushing White House dr. Ronnie Jackson  who said Trump's fine because he was  able to figure out which drawing was of  a giraffe when presented with drawings  of a giraffe and a tiger the upside is  and this is sort of a really weird  silver lining the upside is that Trump  might only be a psychopath and what I  mean by that is that there's a higher  prevalence of psychopathy among  politicians and lots of the signs and  symptoms that I listed are similar to  those of psychopathy  so the best-case scenario here is that  Donald Trump might only be a psychopath
 

